# mkrecipe-probe -- WHICH construct in a tab-indented recipe does rc
# refuse?  One command:
#
#	cd sys/lib/tests/mkrecipe-probe && mk -k all
#
# `-k' is not optional: mk stops at the first failing recipe, and the
# whole point here is to get every answer in one run rather than one
# round each.  Every case that works prints `ok N'; a case that does
# not prints an rc syntax error instead, and the number names the
# construct.  Nothing is compiled and nothing is installed.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS.  `cmd/clisp/mkfile.rebuild' answered
#
#	/rc/lib/rcmain:24 *eval*:13: token '=': syntax error
#
# and the ONLY `=' characters anywhere in that recipe were two inside
# COMMENT lines plus one leading assignment, which rc allows.  So
# either rc is lexing recipe comments after all, or mk is doing
# something to them on the way, or the line rc names is not the line
# that failed.  **Three different causes, three different fixes, and
# reading cannot separate them**: there is no rc on the build host, so
# every statement about rc's lexer in this tree is a recollection until
# a VM run says otherwise -- which is exactly how the `=' rule itself
# sat unmeasured for rounds.
#
# Case 1 is the CONTROL.  A recipe comment with nothing special in it
# must work, and the tree says it already does -- `external/perl/plan9/
# mkfile:88' is a whole-line recipe comment and perl builds.  If case 1
# fails, the answer is "rc sees recipe comments at all" and every
# comment in every recipe in this tree is a hazard.  If case 1 passes
# and case 2 or 3 fails, it is the CONTENT, and the rule is narrower.
#
# Case 5 has a precedent that already works and is here to be excluded
# rather than to be learnt: `cmd/tar/mkfile:126' is
# `echo '#ifndef DEFAULT_RMT_COMMAND' >rmt-command.h' and tar's header
# comes out right, so mk does not strip `#' from inside quotes.  *A
# case whose answer is known is what makes the others readable* -- if
# 5 fails, the probe itself is measuring something other than rc.

all:V: t1 t2 t3 t4 t5 t6

t1:V:
	# a plain comment, nothing special in it
	echo ok 1 plain recipe comment

t2:V:
	# this comment contains a bare = sign
	echo ok 2 comment containing a bare equals

t3:V:
	# `~' rather than `test $name = x': `=' is a SYNTAX CHARACTER
	echo ok 3 comment with backquotes apostrophes and equals

t4:V:
	if (~ x x) {
		echo ok 4a
	} if not {
		echo not reached
	}
	echo ok 4 brace-if-not on one line

t5:V:
	echo abc | sed -e '/^#line /!s/.*//' -e 's/^/ok 5 /'

t6:V:
	@{
		v=hello
		echo ok 6 leading assignment inside a block $v
	}
